{"module":"veterans","voice":"male","duration":186.4,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Black veterans: fighting for a country that fought them","start":0.0,"end":3.45,"words":[[0.04,0.35],[0.35,1.05],[1.32,1.78],[1.78,1.94],[1.94,2.09],[2.09,2.56],[2.56,2.79],[2.79,3.02],[3.02,3.25]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black Americans have served in every U.S. war.","start":4.25,"end":7.7,"words":[[4.31,4.61],[4.61,5.28],[5.28,5.5],[5.5,5.8],[5.8,5.95],[5.95,6.25],[6.25,6.62],[6.99,7.22]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Many came home expecting the freedom they had fought for, and that expectation made them targets.","start":8.05,"end":14.78,"words":[[8.11,8.42],[8.42,8.74],[8.74,9.06],[9.06,9.78],[9.78,9.94],[9.94,10.41],[10.41,10.65],[10.65,10.89],[10.89,11.13],[11.13,11.37],[11.65,11.89],[11.89,12.12],[12.12,13.08],[13.08,13.4],[13.4,13.64],[13.64,14.19]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Revolution and Civil War","start":15.13,"end":17.07,"words":[[15.2,15.99],[15.99,16.25],[16.25,16.69],[16.69,16.87]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent, was the first killed in the Boston Massacre (1770).","start":17.77,"end":25.27,"words":[[17.79,18.3],[18.3,18.74],[18.99,19.14],[19.14,19.36],[19.36,19.51],[19.51,20.02],[20.02,20.24],[20.24,20.67],[20.67,21.19],[21.44,21.66],[21.66,21.81],[21.81,22.1],[22.1,22.39],[22.39,22.54],[22.54,22.68],[22.68,23.12],[23.12,23.56],[23.56,24.73]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Thousands of Black soldiers fought for independence, while others joined the British, who promised freedom to people enslaved by rebels.","start":25.62,"end":34.42,"words":[[25.67,26.27],[26.27,26.41],[26.41,26.71],[26.71,27.31],[27.31,27.53],[27.53,27.68],[27.68,28.58],[28.84,29.14],[29.14,29.43],[29.43,29.88],[29.88,30.03],[30.03,30.55],[30.81,30.96],[30.96,31.49],[31.49,31.93],[31.93,32.08],[32.08,32.45],[32.45,33.05],[33.05,33.27],[33.27,33.8]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the Civil War, about 179,000 Black men served in the Union Army.","start":34.77,"end":40.18,"words":[[34.82,34.95],[34.95,35.08],[35.08,35.4],[35.4,35.59],[35.82,36.14],[36.14,38.08],[38.08,38.34],[38.34,38.53],[38.53,38.79],[38.79,38.92],[38.92,39.05],[39.05,39.44],[39.44,39.69]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Confederates massacred surrendering Black soldiers at Fort Pillow in 1864.","start":40.53,"end":45.93,"words":[[40.58,41.38],[41.38,41.89],[41.89,42.62],[42.62,42.91],[42.91,43.49],[43.49,43.64],[43.64,43.86],[43.86,44.22],[44.22,44.36],[44.36,45.38]]},{"el":"h1","text":"World War I and the backlash","start":46.83,"end":49.34,"words":[[46.88,47.35],[47.35,47.59],[47.59,47.82],[47.82,48.18],[48.18,48.41],[48.41,49.12]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The 369th Infantry, the “Harlem Hellfighters,” spent more time in combat than almost any other American unit.","start":50.04,"end":58.43,"words":[[50.09,50.24],[50.24,51.98],[51.98,52.66],[52.92,53.08],[53.08,53.45],[53.45,54.21],[54.21,54.59],[54.59,54.74],[54.74,55.04],[55.04,55.19],[55.19,55.65],[55.65,55.88],[55.88,56.41],[56.41,56.63],[56.63,56.86],[56.86,57.46],[57.46,57.92]]},{"el":"s1","text":"At home, the Army hanged 19 Black soldiers of the 24th Infantry after the 1917 Houston riot, 13 of them in a single day without appeal; in 2023 the Army set aside the convictions of 110 of the soldiers.","start":58.78,"end":74.6,"words":[[58.83,58.98],[58.98,59.35],[59.6,59.75],[59.75,59.97],[59.97,60.27],[60.27,60.78],[60.78,61.08],[61.08,61.67],[61.67,61.81],[61.81,61.96],[61.96,62.62],[62.62,63.21],[63.21,63.51],[63.51,63.66],[63.66,64.76],[64.76,65.28],[65.28,65.72],[65.98,66.35],[66.35,66.49],[66.49,66.71],[66.71,66.86],[66.86,67.01],[67.01,67.45],[67.45,67.67],[67.67,68.11],[68.11,68.48],[68.74,68.89],[68.89,69.99],[69.99,70.14],[70.14,70.36],[70.36,70.58],[70.58,70.95],[70.95,71.1],[71.1,71.84],[71.84,71.98],[71.98,73.09],[73.09,73.24],[73.24,73.38],[73.38,74.05]]},{"el":"s1","text":"After the war, Black veterans were beaten and lynched, some in uniform, during the Red Summer of 1919 (EJI, Targeting Black Veterans).","start":74.95,"end":85.36,"words":[[75.0,75.32],[75.32,75.48],[75.48,75.72],[76.0,76.32],[76.32,76.96],[76.96,77.12],[77.12,77.52],[77.52,77.76],[77.76,78.32],[78.6,78.84],[78.84,79.0],[79.0,79.64],[79.92,80.48],[80.48,80.64],[80.64,80.88],[80.88,81.2],[81.2,81.36],[81.36,82.47],[82.47,82.87],[83.15,83.71],[83.71,84.03],[84.03,84.75]]},{"el":"h2","text":"World War II and “Double V”","start":86.26,"end":88.86,"words":[[86.31,86.71],[86.71,86.92],[86.92,87.73],[87.73,88.03],[88.03,88.54],[88.54,88.74]]},{"el":"s2","text":"More than a million Black Americans served in World War II, including the Tuskegee Airmen.","start":89.56,"end":96.04,"words":[[89.6,89.76],[89.76,89.99],[89.99,90.15],[90.15,90.62],[90.62,90.94],[90.94,91.65],[91.65,91.96],[91.96,92.12],[92.12,92.43],[92.43,92.59],[92.59,93.3],[93.58,94.21],[94.21,94.37],[94.37,95.0],[95.0,95.47]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Pittsburgh Courier's “Double V” campaign called for victory over fascism abroad and racism at home.","start":96.39,"end":103.84,"words":[[96.44,96.61],[96.61,97.24],[97.24,97.86],[97.86,98.31],[98.31,98.48],[98.48,99.11],[99.11,99.46],[99.46,99.64],[99.64,100.27],[100.27,100.62],[100.62,101.25],[101.25,101.69],[101.69,101.96],[101.96,102.67],[102.67,102.85],[102.85,103.29]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1944 an explosion at Port Chicago, California, killed 320 men, most of them Black sailors loading ammunition; 50 who refused to return to the same unsafe work were convicted of mutiny.","start":104.19,"end":120.69,"words":[[104.24,104.41],[104.41,105.52],[105.52,105.69],[105.69,106.55],[106.55,106.72],[106.72,106.97],[106.97,107.66],[107.96,108.81],[109.11,109.45],[109.45,110.99],[110.99,111.33],[111.63,112.06],[112.06,112.23],[112.23,112.48],[112.48,112.83],[112.83,113.34],[113.34,113.85],[113.85,114.71],[115.0,115.43],[115.43,115.6],[115.6,116.2],[116.2,116.37],[116.37,116.8],[116.8,116.97],[116.97,117.14],[117.14,117.48],[117.48,117.99],[117.99,118.25],[118.25,118.42],[118.42,119.19],[119.19,119.36],[119.36,120.05]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Navy exonerated them in 2024.","start":121.04,"end":124.44,"words":[[121.09,121.23],[121.23,121.6],[121.6,122.47],[122.47,122.69],[122.69,122.84],[122.84,123.93]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Isaac Woodard and desegregation","start":125.34,"end":127.38,"words":[[125.39,125.73],[125.73,126.08],[126.08,126.29],[126.29,127.18]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In February 1946, hours after his honorable discharge, Sergeant Isaac Woodard was pulled off a bus in Batesburg, South Carolina, and beaten by the police chief until he was blind.","start":128.08,"end":140.34,"words":[[128.13,128.28],[128.28,128.85],[128.85,130.0],[130.25,130.54],[130.54,130.83],[130.83,131.05],[131.05,131.62],[131.62,132.27],[132.52,133.02],[133.02,133.38],[133.38,133.74],[133.74,133.96],[133.96,134.25],[134.25,134.39],[134.39,134.53],[134.53,134.75],[134.75,134.89],[134.89,135.54],[135.79,136.08],[136.08,136.8],[137.05,137.27],[137.27,137.63],[137.63,137.84],[137.84,137.99],[137.99,138.34],[138.34,138.63],[138.63,138.99],[138.99,139.14],[139.14,139.35],[139.35,139.86]]},{"el":"s3","text":"An all-white jury acquitted the chief.","start":140.69,"end":142.92,"words":[[140.73,140.85],[140.85,141.22],[141.22,141.58],[141.58,142.0],[142.0,142.12],[142.12,142.43]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Outrage over the case helped move President Truman to order the desegregation of the armed forces in 1948 (Executive Order 9981).","start":143.27,"end":153.24,"words":[[143.32,143.88],[143.88,144.16],[144.16,144.3],[144.3,144.58],[144.58,144.93],[144.93,145.14],[145.14,145.77],[145.77,146.19],[146.19,146.32],[146.32,146.53],[146.53,146.67],[146.67,147.58],[147.58,147.72],[147.72,147.86],[147.86,148.07],[148.07,148.42],[148.42,148.56],[148.56,149.61],[149.61,150.31],[150.31,150.52],[150.52,152.75]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many civil rights leaders, including Medgar Evers, were veterans; Tulsa's defenders in 1921 were veterans too.","start":153.59,"end":162.15,"words":[[153.63,153.94],[153.94,154.31],[154.31,154.69],[154.69,155.15],[155.41,156.01],[156.01,156.39],[156.39,156.77],[157.03,157.18],[157.18,157.86],[158.13,158.58],[158.58,159.19],[159.19,159.34],[159.34,160.55],[160.55,160.7],[160.7,161.3],[161.3,161.53]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Recognition delayed","start":163.05,"end":164.71,"words":[[163.14,164.01],[164.01,164.54]]},{"el":"s4","text":"No Black soldier received the Medal of Honor for World War II until 1997, when seven were finally awarded it after a review; only one, Vernon Baker, was still alive.","start":165.41,"end":179.09,"words":[[165.46,165.71],[165.71,166.05],[166.05,166.64],[166.64,167.14],[167.14,167.31],[167.31,167.73],[167.73,167.9],[167.9,168.15],[168.15,168.32],[168.32,168.65],[168.65,168.82],[168.82,169.49],[169.49,169.92],[169.92,171.51],[171.81,172.06],[172.06,172.48],[172.48,172.65],[172.65,173.23],[173.23,173.74],[173.74,173.91],[173.91,174.24],[174.24,174.41],[174.41,174.92],[175.21,175.63],[175.63,175.97],[176.26,176.68],[176.68,177.18],[177.48,177.73],[177.73,178.07],[178.07,178.57]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black veterans were also largely shut out of the GI Bill's benefits (see the module on wealth).","start":179.44,"end":185.48,"words":[[179.49,179.8],[179.8,180.42],[180.42,180.58],[180.58,180.97],[180.97,181.44],[181.44,181.67],[181.67,181.9],[181.9,182.06],[182.06,182.22],[182.22,182.68],[182.68,183.0],[183.0,183.62],[183.62,183.78],[183.78,183.93],[183.93,184.4],[184.4,184.56],[184.56,184.94]]}]}